-- Scene 1: THE KILL CRITERION -- a 10 ms ramp does not click --
   A steady 440 Hz tone through a gain node. Its own steepest
   sample-to-sample slope is the yardstick: any gain change that jumps
   produces a delta far larger, and that delta IS the click.
   the tone's own steepest step: 0.0288
   with NO CHANGE   : worst step 0.0288   <- the instrument's own noise
  [ok]   an unchanged gain produces no step beyond the tone's own slope
   with NO ramp     : worst step 0.3744
  [ok]   an unramped gain change DOES click -- the control works
   with a 10 ms ramp: worst step 0.0288
   the unramped jump is 13.0211x the ramped step
  [ok]   a 10 ms ramp does NOT click -- no step exceeds the tone's own slope
  [ok]   and it is dramatically smaller than the unramped change

   VERDICT: the criterion is MET. Ducking ships ramped; it would have been
   dropped in favour of drop-and-count if this number had gone the other way.
   the gain the render is applying afterwards: 0.2500
  [ok]   the ramp ARRIVES at its target rather than creeping
   peak before 0.5000, peak after the ramp 0.1250
  [ok]   and the SOUND is actually attenuated, not just the number

